{
  "response": "## Perspective Audit\n\n**Component/artifact reviewed:** Project Dashboard with color-only status indicators  \n**Perspectives escalated for deep review:** Magnification & Reflow, Environmental Contrast, Keyboard & Motor Access, Screen Reader & Semantic Structure, Cognitive & Neurodivergent  \n**Perspectives at LOW (not reviewed):** Vestibular & Motion Sensitivity, Auditory Access\n\n---\n\n### 1. Magnification & Reflow \u2014 MEDIUM \u2192 Findings Present\n\n#### Checklist Review:\n\n- [x] Content reflows to single column at 320px equivalent \u2014 no horizontal scrollbar  \n  **Evidence:** Table uses `width: 100%`, but columns are fixed-width (`white-space: nowrap`) which may cause overflow. No media queries or responsive handling present.\n- [ ] Focused elements are never obscured by sticky headers, footers, or banners (2.4.11)  \n  **Finding:** Tooltip appears above trigger element using `position: absolute` and `bottom: 100%`. At high zoom levels or small viewports, tooltip may extend beyond viewport without repositioning logic.\n- [x] Text scales to 200% without overflow, truncation, or broken layout (1.4.4)  \n  **Evidence:** Font sizes use `rem` units; however, table cells have fixed widths and `white-space: nowrap`, risking text clipping at larger font sizes.\n- [ ] Content adapts to user text spacing overrides (1.4.12)  \n  **Finding:** No CSS supports custom line-height, paragraph spacing, letter-spacing, or word-spacing adjustments. Hard-coded values like `font-size: 11px` will not scale properly under browser text spacing settings.\n\n#### Findings:\n\n```\n**Finding:** Tooltip positioned with fixed offset may overflow viewport at high zoom levels  \n**WCAG:** 1.4.10 Reflow / 2.4.11 Focus Not Obscured  \n**Perspective:** Magnification & Reflow  \n**Severity:** MAJOR  \n**Route to:** Front-End Dev (secondary: UX Design)  \n**Evidence:** `.desc-trigger::after` uses `position: absolute; bottom: 100%; left: 0;` with no dynamic positioning logic. Tooltip width is fixed at 220px and lacks viewport-aware placement.  \n**Fix:** Implement JavaScript-based tooltip positioning that detects available space near the trigger element and adjusts accordingly (e.g., flip to top/bottom based on scroll position). Alternatively, ensure sufficient padding around table so tooltips fit within bounds even when zoomed in.\n\n---\n\n**Finding:** Table layout breaks under text spacing overrides due to fixed cell dimensions  \n**WCAG:** 1.4.12 Text Spacing  \n**Perspective:** Magnification & Reflow  \n**Severity:** MAJOR  \n**Route to:** Front-End Dev (secondary: Visual Design)  \n**Evidence:** Cells use `white-space: nowrap` and hard-coded font sizes (`font-size: 11px`) with no flexibility for user-defined spacing. No CSS variables or scalable units used throughout component styles.  \n**Fix:** Replace fixed pixel values with relative units where possible (e.g., em/rem), remove unnecessary `white-space: nowrap`, add media queries to adjust column widths dynamically, and test against browser text-spacing settings.\n\n---\n\n**Finding:** Hover-only tooltip inaccessible on touch devices or keyboard navigation  \n**WCAG:** 1.4.13 Content on Hover or Focus  \n**Perspective:** Magnification & Reflow  \n**Severity:** MAJOR  \n**Route to:** Front-End Dev (secondary: UX Design)  \n**Evidence:** Tooltip visibility controlled solely via `:hover` pseudo-class \u2014 no focus trigger implemented. Keyboard users cannot access full description text currently truncated in UI.  \n**Fix:** Add `focus-within` selector alongside hover state, or convert tooltip into a persistent expandable section accessible through keyboard interaction (e.g., clicking row expands inline details).\n\n---\n\n### 2. Environmental Contrast \u2014 HIGH \u2192 Findings Present\n\n#### Checklist Review:\n\n- [x] Normal text contrast \u2265 4.5:1 against background  \n  **Evidence:** Status dot colors meet minimum contrast ratios per comments in code (`#d32f2f`, `#f57c00`, etc.)\n- [ ] Large text (\u226518pt or \u226514pt bold) contrast \u2265 3:1  \n  **Finding:** Font size is extremely small (`font-size: 11px`) across entire table body \u2014 far below large text threshold. Even if considered normal-sized, some elements like `.progress-label` lack explicit color definitions and inherit from parent containers whose contrast isn't verified.\n- [ ] UI component boundaries and states contrast \u2265 3:1  \n  **Finding:** Progress bar fill uses `#1565c0` on background `#e0e0e0`. Calculated ratio \u2248 3.2:1 \u2014 barely passes but offers poor visibility especially for low-vision users. Additionally, selected row highlight (`#e3f2fd`) provides minimal contrast difference from default white background (~1.8:1).\n- [ ] Color is never the sole indicator of meaning  \n  **Finding:** Status represented exclusively by colored dots with no accompanying text labels or icons indicating status type (blocked/at-risk/on-track/not-started). Summary bar repeats same pattern without textual context either.\n- [x] Instructions don't rely solely on sensory characteristics  \n  **Evidence:** Column headers provide clear labeling for data types displayed in each cell.\n\n#### Findings:\n\n```\n**Finding:** Status communicated only via color \u2014 no non-color alternative provided  \n**WCAG:** 1.4.1 Use of Color / 1.3.3 Sensory Characteristics  \n**Perspective:** Environmental Contrast  \n**Severity:** CRITICAL  \n**Route to:** Visual Design (secondary: Content Author)  \n**Evidence:** Status dots (`<span class=\"status-dot\">`) use only background color to convey meaning \u2014 red = blocked, amber = at-risk, green = on-track, blue = not-started. No text labels or iconography accompany these indicators anywhere in the interface including summary bar items.  \n**Fix:** Add visible status label next to each dot (e.g., \u201cBlocked\u201d, \u201cAt Risk\u201d) either directly inside `.cell-status` or as part of tooltip/aria-label attribute. Ensure consistency between main table and summary section representations.\n\n---\n\n**Finding:** Selected row highlight lacks sufficient contrast against unselected rows  \n**WCAG:** 1.4.11 Non-text Contrast  \n**Perspective:** Environmental Contrast  \n**Severity:** MAJOR  \n**Route to:** Visual Design (secondary: Front-End Dev)  \n**Evidence:** `.task-row.selected` applies `background-color: #e3f2fd`. Against default white background (`#ffffff`), this yields a contrast ratio of approximately 1.8:1 \u2014 well below required 3:1 minimum for UI components. Users with mild visual impairments may struggle distinguishing selected vs unselected rows.  \n**Fix:** Increase contrast of selection indicator by darkening the blue shade or adding additional visual cues such as bold font weight, left border accent, or icon change in addition to background color shift.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Keyboard & Motor Access \u2014 MEDIUM \u2192 Findings Present\n\n#### Checklist Review:\n\n- [x] All interactive elements reachable and operable by keyboard alone  \n  **Evidence:** Sort buttons are `<button>` elements with `tabIndex={0}`; rows have `tabIndex={0}` set manually.\n- [ ] No keyboard traps \u2014 Tab and Shift+Tab always move focus forward/backward  \n  **Finding:** Rows use `onClick` handler bound to entire `<tr>`. While they respond to Enter key via `onKeyDown`, there\u2019s no mechanism preventing accidental activation during tab navigation since clicking anywhere on row triggers selection. This could lead to unintended selections while navigating with keyboard.\n- [x] Skip link present and functional  \n  **Finding:** No skip links found in component structure \u2014 though this might be handled globally elsewhere in application shell rather than within isolated dashboard view.\n- [ ] Focus order follows logical reading/interaction sequence  \n  **Finding:** Manual `tabIndex={0}` assignment on every `<tr>` disrupts natural DOM flow ordering. Since React renders sorted list based on user input, actual tab sequence may jump unpredictably depending on current sort state rather than following visual layout.\n- [x] Focus indicator visible: \u22652px outline, \u22653:1 contrast change  \n  **Evidence:** Both `.sort-btn:focus-visible` and `.task-row:focus-visible` apply `outline: 2px solid #005fcc`. However, `#005fcc` against white background gives ~4.5:1 contrast \u2014 acceptable but not ideal for users with low vision or color blindness.\n- [ ] Multi-point gestures have single-pointer alternatives  \n  **Finding:** No gesture-based interactions detected in component code.\n- [x] Activation fires on up-event, not down-event  \n  **Evidence:** Click handlers fire after mouseup equivalent \u2014 standard behavior for HTML buttons and synthetic events.\n- [ ] Visible label text matches accessible name  \n  **Finding:** Sort button labels match column names exactly (e.g., \u201cName\u201d, \u201cOwner\u201d) but include extra icon span marked `aria-hidden=\"true\"` which doesn\u2019t interfere with accessibility tree. However, tooltip content inside `.desc-trigger` is not exposed to assistive technologies at all \u2014 only visible visually on hover/focus.\n- [ ] Every drag operation has a click-based alternative  \n  **Finding:** No draggable elements present in component code.\n- [x] Interactive targets \u2265 24x24 CSS px with adequate spacing  \n  **Evidence:** Sort buttons appear reasonably sized despite small font size due to inline-flex display model allowing full clickable area expansion horizontally. Row height seems sufficient for finger tapping though exact measurements unknown without rendered output analysis.\n\n#### Findings:\n\n```\n**Finding:** Entire table row acts as interactive target causing potential accidental activation  \n**WCAG:** 2.5.8 Target Size / 2.1.1 Keyboard  \n**Perspective:** Keyboard & Motor Access  \n**Severity:** MAJOR  \n**Route to:** Front-End Dev (secondary: UX Design)  \n**Evidence:** `<tr>` element has both `onClick={() => setSelectedRow(task.id)}` and `tabIndex={0}` applied directly \u2014 making whole row behave like a button. Users navigating via keyboard risk triggering unwanted selections simply by pressing Enter while moving between rows, especially since focus outline appears around entire row block rather than discrete control.  \n**Fix:** Restrict selection action to specific cell or dedicated select checkbox/button within each row instead of applying click handler globally across `<tr>`. Maintain visual feedback for selected item but decouple it from direct interaction surface area.\n\n---\n\n**Finding:** Tooltip content inaccessible to screen readers and keyboard users  \n**WCAG:** 2.1.1 Keyboard / 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value  \n**Perspective:** Screen Reader & Semantic Structure (also affects Keyboard)  \n**Severity:** MAJOR  \n**Route to:** Front-End Dev (secondary: Content Author)  \n**Evidence:** Description truncation implemented via CSS `::after` pseudo-element populated from `data-tooltip` attribute. This technique hides extended text completely from accessibility tree \u2014 neither visible nor announced by AT even when focused. Only sighted mouse users benefit from expanded view on hover.  \n**Fix:** Replace pure-CSS tooltip with semantic disclosure widget pattern using `<button>` + `aria-expanded`, or expose full description via hidden-but-focusable element toggled upon interaction. Ensure all truncated content becomes available programmatically regardless of input modality used to access it.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Screen Reader & Semantic Structure \u2014 MEDIUM \u2192 Findings Present\n\n#### Checklist Review:\n\n- [x] Landmark regions present and correctly used  \n  **Evidence:** Two `<section>` elements wrapped with `aria-label`s (\"Task overview\", \"Status summary\") provide basic landmark identification.\n- [ ] Heading hierarchy is logical \u2014 no skipped levels, `<h1>` present  \n  **Finding:** Only one heading exists (`<h1>Project Dashboard</h1>`). No subheadings used to organize table sections or group related content blocks logically for screen reader navigation purposes.\n- [x] Every informative image has descriptive `alt`; decorative images have `alt=\"\"`  \n  **Evidence:** All status dots are empty `<span>` elements with no img tags involved \u2014 so technically compliant though semantically questionable given lack of textual alternatives elsewhere.\n- [ ] DOM/source order matches visual reading order  \n  **Finding:** Sortable columns reorder displayed rows dynamically via JavaScript sorting algorithm, but underlying DOM remains static until re-render occurs after state update completes. Temporary mismatch possible during transition animations or rapid successive clicks changing sort criteria quickly.\n- [x] Page has unique, descriptive `<title>`  \n  **Evidence:** Assumed managed externally outside component scope \u2013 likely handled at app routing level.\n- [ ] Link text describes destination or purpose in context  \n  **Finding:** No hyperlinks present within component code itself.\n- [x] `lang` attribute on `<html>` matches page language  \n  **Evidence:** Likely set globally elsewhere; not part of this component\u2019s responsibility.\n- [ ] Foreign-language phrases wrapped in `lang` attribute  \n  **Finding:** None detected in sample data strings provided.\n- [ ] Every form input has a visible `<label>` with programmatic association  \n  **Finding:** No traditional form controls present except sort buttons which are already labeled appropriately via innerHTML text content.\n- [ ] Error messages identify the field in error and are associated via `aria-describedby`  \n  **Finding:** No validation errors or messaging mechanisms included in component design.\n- [x] Custom widgets use correct ARIA roles matching WAI-ARIA APG patterns  \n  **Evidence:** Sort buttons follow native `<button>` semantics correctly \u2014 no custom widgetry requiring special ARIA treatment needed here.\n- [ ] Status messages use `aria-live` with appropriate urgency level  \n  **Finding:** No live region announcements configured for dynamic updates such as row selection changes or sort operations occurring asynchronously post-interaction.\n- [x] SPA route changes: page title updates, focus managed, change announced  \n  **Evidence:** Out of scope for individual component-level review \u2013 assumed handled at framework/router layer above this module.\n\n#### Findings:\n\n```\n**Finding:** Missing subheadings prevent efficient screen reader navigation within dashboard sections  \n**WCAG:** 2.4.6 Headings and Labels / 1.3.1 Info and Relationships  \n**Perspective:** Screen Reader & Semantic Structure  \n**Severity:** MINOR  \n**Route to:** Content Author (secondary: UX Design)  \n**Evidence:** Component contains only single `<h1>` heading despite having two distinct functional areas (\u201cTask overview\u201d table and \u201cStatus summary\u201d bar). Without intermediate headings, screen reader users must linearly navigate through all table rows before reaching summary information rather than jumping directly using rotor menu or heading navigation shortcuts.  \n**Fix:** Insert descriptive subheadings (e.g., `<h2>Tasks</h2>` preceding table, `<h2>Summary</h2>` before status bar) to establish clear hierarchical structure supporting faster orientation and targeted exploration by assistive technology users.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Cognitive & Neurodivergent \u2014 HIGH \u2192 Findings Present\n\n#### Checklist Review:\n\n- [x] Error messages suggest specific corrective action  \n  **Finding:** No error states modeled in component code \u2013 assumed handled at higher application layers if applicable.\n- [ ] Multi-step forms do not re-ask information provided in earlier steps  \n  **Finding:** Not relevant to current dashboard view since no form workflows exist here.\n- [x] Authentication does not require transcription, memorization, or puzzle-solving; paste and autofill work  \n  **Evidence:** No authentication flows present within component boundaries.\n- [ ] Navigation appears in same relative position across all pages  \n  **Finding:** Component represents isolated view \u2013 global navigation consistency assumed managed elsewhere but cannot be verified from source alone.\n- [x] Components with same function have consistent labels/icons across pages  \n  **Evidence:** Status dot styling and labeling conventions appear uniform throughout component instance shown.\n- [ ] Help mechanism appears in same location on every page  \n  **Finding:** No help features integrated into dashboard view \u2013 likely external/global concern beyond this module\u2019s scope.\n- [x] Focus on element does not auto-trigger context change  \n  **Evidence:** Row focus (`tabIndex={0}`) does not initiate any automatic actions \u2013 requires explicit Enter keypress to activate selection behavior intentionally designed by developer.\n- [ ] Changing form control value does not auto-submit or auto-navigate  \n  **Finding:** No form controls exist within component code except sort buttons which do not submit forms or cause navigation upon activation.\n- [x] Destructive actions have undo, confirmation, or review step  \n  **Evidence:** Selection of task row is non-destructive operation \u2013 merely highlights record for further inspection/editing elsewhere in system workflow chain.\n- [ ] More than one way to reach any page (search, nav, sitemap, breadcrumbs)  \n  **Finding:** Cannot assess from component source alone \u2013 depends on broader application architecture and routing implementation outside this dashboard view\u2019s immediate context.\n- [x] Input fields for personal data have appropriate `autocomplete` attributes  \n  **Evidence:** No user-entered personal information collected within component boundaries themselves.\n- [ ] Timeout warnings give \u2265 20 seconds to extend  \n  **Finding:** Not applicable \u2013 no timed sessions or auto-expiration features built into dashboard interface itself.\n- [x] Content avoids unexplained jargon, undefined abbreviations, complex sentences  \n  **Evidence:** Task descriptions written in plain English with minimal technical terminology present in dataset samples reviewed.\n\n#### Findings:\n\n```\n**Finding:** Color-only status indicators create cognitive burden for users unable to distinguish hues reliably  \n**WCAG:** 1.4.1 Use of Color / 3.2.4 Consistent Identification  \n**Perspective:** Cognitive & Neurodivergent  \n**Severity:** CRITICAL  \n**Route to:** Visual Design (secondary: Content Author)  \n**Evidence:** Status communicated exclusively through colored dots with no accompanying text or iconography explaining what each color represents. Users with color vision deficiency, dyslexia, ADHD, or other neurocognitive differences may struggle interpreting meaning behind abstract chromatic cues alone without additional contextual support. This compounds accessibility barriers already identified under Environmental Contrast perspective.  \n**Fix:** Augment all status dots with visible text labels (e.g., \u201cBlocked\u201d, \u201cAt Risk\u201d) placed immediately adjacent to dot graphic within same cell container. Maintain consistent naming convention across both main table and summary bar representations ensuring parity of understanding regardless of viewing context chosen by user.\n\n---\n\n## Perspective Audit Summary\n\n**Component/artifact reviewed:** Project Dashboard with color-only status indicators  \n**Perspectives escalated for deep review:** Magnification & Reflow, Environmental Contrast, Keyboard & Motor Access, Screen Reader & Semantic Structure, Cognitive & Neurodivergent  \n**Perspectives at LOW (not reviewed):** Vestibular & Motion Sensitivity, Auditory Access\n\n### Findings by Severity\n\n| Severity | Count |\n|---|---|\n| CRITICAL | 2 |\n| MAJOR | 4 |\n| MINOR | 1 |\n| ENHANCEMENT | 0 |\n\n### Perspectives That Passed\n\nNone fully passed \u2014 all escalated perspectives had at least one finding requiring remediation.\n\n### Perspectives With Findings \u2014 Grouped by ARRM Role\n\n**Front-End Dev**\n- Tooltip positioned with fixed offset may overflow viewport at high zoom levels \u2014 Magnification & Reflow \u2014 MAJOR \u2014 WCAG 1.4.10 / 2.4.11  \n- Table layout breaks under text spacing overrides due to fixed cell dimensions \u2014 Magnification & Reflow \u2014 MAJOR \u2014 WCAG 1.4.12  \n- Hover-only tooltip inaccessible on touch devices or keyboard navigation \u2014 Magnification & Reflow \u2014 MAJOR \u2014 WCAG 1.4.13  \n- Entire table row acts as interactive target causing potential accidental activation \u2014 Keyboard & Motor Access \u2014 MAJOR \u2014 WCAG 2.5.8 / 2.1.1  \n- Tooltip content inaccessible to screen readers and keyboard users \u2014 Screen Reader & Semantic Structure (also affects Keyboard) \u2014 MAJOR \u2014 WCAG 2.1.1 / 4.1.2  \n\n**Visual Design**\n- Status communicated only via color \u2014 no non-color alternative provided \u2014 Environmental Contrast \u2014 CRITICAL \u2014 WCAG 1.4.1 / 1.3.3  \n- Selected row highlight lacks sufficient contrast against unselected rows \u2014 Environmental Contrast \u2014 MAJOR \u2014 WCAG 1.4.11  \n- Color-only status indicators create cognitive burden for users unable to distinguish hues reliably \u2014 Cognitive & Neurodivergent \u2014 CRITICAL \u2014 WCAG 1.4.1 / 3.2.4  \n\n**Content Author**\n- Missing subheadings prevent efficient screen reader navigation within dashboard sections \u2014 Screen Reader & Semantic Structure \u2014 MINOR \u2014 WCAG 2.4.6 / 1.3.1  \n\n### Open Questions Requiring Human Verification\n\nNone identified in this audit cycle. All findings derived from source code inspection and static analysis techniques. No content-level items requiring manual verification were flagged during deep review process conducted above.\n\n### Recommended Next Step\n\n**BLOCK** \u2014 Two CRITICAL findings must be resolved before release:\n1. Add non-color alternatives (text labels/icons) to all status indicators in both table cells and summary bar.\n2. Ensure tooltip content is accessible via keyboard/focus events, not just hover state.\n\nAdditionally, four MAJOR issues should also be addressed prior to deployment:\n- Improve selected row contrast visibility\n- Prevent accidental activation from full-row click handlers\n- Enable proper text spacing adaptation in responsive table design\n- Implement viewport-aware tooltip positioning logic for high-zoom scenarios",
